+## Subtopics
+
+### [Histogram Peak Interpolation for Transfer Function Generation](16869-histogram-peak-interpolation-for-transfer-function-generatio.md)
+A method for automatically generating intermediate transfer functions by analyzing the histogram data between two existing unit transfer function peaks. It distinguishes itself from its parent method by focusing on the interval between peaks rather than the global histogram maximums, and from its child, Visualization by Example, by providing the underlying algorithmic logic for peak detection rather than a constructive interface.
+
+### Search Around a Feature
+An operation that finds peaks in the histogram surrounding the peak corresponding to a selected visualization's transfer function, creating new unit transfer functions for them. Unlike the initial peak selection which sorts by importance (height relative to valleys), this function selects any peak regardless of its size and height [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** The Douglas-Peucker algorithm has a worst-case time complexity of $O(n^2)$, where $n$ is the number of points in the histogram. However, it often performs closer to $O(n \log n)$ on typical data. Histogram generation is $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels.
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ for storing the histogram and simplified curve.
-**Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-**Scaling:** The method scales with the number of voxels for histogram generation and the number of bins for the Douglas-Peucker algorithm.
-**Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides a good basis for exploration by generating simple, recognizable visualizations; allows users to assemble complex visualizations from simple units.
-**Limitations:** Assumes a specific relationship between density and opacity (higher density = inside); may not be suitable for all data types; relies on the choice of $\epsilon$.
-**When to use:** When the data has clear peaks in the histogram corresponding to distinct structures, and the user wants to quickly generate simple visualizations for exploration.
-**When not to use:** When the data does not have clear peaks, or when the assumption about density and opacity does not hold.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The Douglas-Peucker algorithm typically runs in $O(n \log n)$ on average, where $n$ is the number of histogram bins, though worst-case is $O(n^2)$. Sorting peaks is $O(k \log k)$ where $k$ is the number of peaks.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ to store the histogram and polyline points.
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces complex transfer function design to a few simple, recognizable components. Automates the discovery of major material boundaries.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that structures correspond to histogram peaks. The linear opacity model is restrictive and data-type dependent. The choice of $\epsilon$ significantly impacts the result and requires user tuning.
+*   **When to use:** When the dataset has distinct material classes with clear density separations (e.g., medical CT).
+*   **When not to use:** When the data lacks clear histogram peaks or when the density-to-opacity relationship is non-linear or inverted.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in 2010 by SCITEPRESS [S1, unofficial]. It builds on previous research demonstrating that structures in scalar volume data sets can often be characterized by the first and second order derivatives [S1, unofficial]. The Douglas-Peucker algorithm was originally proposed by Peucker in 1973 [S1, unofficial]. The method is used in the context of a spreadsheet-like interface for "programming-by-example" in volume rendering [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in the context of "Visualization by Example," a constructive interface for direct volume rendering. It builds on the observation that structures in scalar volume data sets can be characterized by peaks in the histogram, corresponding to the size of the structures [S1, unofficial]. The approach uses the Douglas-Peucker algorithm, originally from cartography (Peucker, 1973), to simplify the histogram curve [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a spreadsheet-like interface where users assemble complex visualizations from these unit transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Sources
